---
import DocsPage from "../../components/DocsPage.astro";

const description = "ChartKit's MCP server validates, repairs, and renders a spec, and looks up feature support.";
const cursor = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "chartkit": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@chartkitdev/mcp"]
    }
  }
}`;
const local = `npm run build --workspace=@chartkitdev/mcp
node packages/mcp/dist/cli.js`;
---

<DocsPage title="MCP server" description={description} href="/docs/mcp">
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Install</strong>
    <code>npx -y @chartkitdev/mcp</code>
  </div>
  <p>
    The server speaks stdio. <code>validate</code> and <code>repair</code> come from <code>@chartkitdev/core/validate</code>. <code>render</code> uses <code>@chartkitdev/core/extra</code>, which includes every chart type. Those tools do not call the network. <code>render_chart</code> does: it asks ChartKit Cloud for an image URL. <code>lookup_feature</code> reads a build-time copy of <a href="/features.json">features.json</a>, synonyms included. A <code>partial</code> cell is not reported as native. Other libraries come back with ChartKit.
  </p>
  <h2>Tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>validate(spec)</code> returns the core diagnostics, including each JSON Patch fix.</li>
    <li><code>repair(spec)</code> applies those fixes and returns the repaired spec plus the remaining diagnostics.</li>
    <li><code>render(spec, {'{ format: "svg" }'})</code> returns the SVG string, plus width and height. PNG is not implemented on this local tool. A rasterizer would add a native or wasm dependency, and this package does not include one.</li>
    <li>
      <code>render_chart(spec)</code> calls ChartKit Cloud and returns the image URL plus the repairs and warnings. Omit <code>format</code>, or pass <code>svg</code>. Pass <code>png</code> only when the host cannot display SVG, such as email, a GitHub README image, or some Slack clients, and then set <code>scale</code> to 2 or higher. The chart background defaults to <code>#ffffff</code>. Pass <code>background</code> (a hex color, or <code>transparent</code>) to override it. Use <code>theme: "midnight"</code> when the host is dark. Set <code>CHARTKIT_CLOUD_KEY</code> to remove the mark. Send it as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>. The image URL does not contain it. This is the one tool that uses the network. See <a href="/docs/cloud">Cloud render</a>.
    </li>
    <li><code>lookup_feature(query)</code> searches the matrix. The result has ChartKit's status, the spec snippet when the matrix has one, the docs URL, and the other libraries.</li>
    <li><code>list_chart_types()</code> and <code>get_example(type)</code> return the example specs from the repo.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Resources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>chartkit://llms.txt</code></li>
    <li><code>chartkit://schema/v1.json</code></li>
  </ul>
  <h2>From a checkout</h2>
  <p>This works before the publish. The process is an MCP server on stdin and stdout.</p>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>shell</figcaption><pre><code>{local}</code></pre></figure>
  <h2>Cursor</h2>
  <p>After publish, add this to <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>. <code>npx</code> cannot download the package until then.</p>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>json</figcaption><pre><code>{cursor}</code></pre></figure>
  <h2>Claude Desktop</h2>
  <p>
    The same <code>mcpServers</code> block goes in <code>claude_desktop_config.json</code> (macOS: <code>~/Library/Application Support/Claude/</code>, Windows: <code>%APPDATA%\Claude\</code>, Linux: <code>~/.config/Claude/</code>).
  </p>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>json</figcaption><pre><code>{cursor}</code></pre></figure>
  <h2>Claude Code</h2>
  <p>After publish, or commit the JSON above as <code>.mcp.json</code>:</p>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>shell</figcaption><pre><code>claude mcp add chartkit -- npx -y @chartkitdev/mcp</code></pre></figure>
</DocsPage>
